        <h3 id="guests">If you are a guest on an interview show</h3>
        <p>The Age of AI and Nerra Voices interview real people, so a guest shares more with us than a listener does. This is all of it.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Your application.</strong> Your name, email, and anything else you fill in on the apply form: phone number, role, organisation, a short bio, topics, links, how long and how deep you would like the interview to go, points to cover, anything off limits, and your rough availability.</li>
            <li><strong>Your booking.</strong> You pick a time through Cal.com, which receives your name, email and the slot you chose.</li>
            <li><strong>Emails and a reminder text.</strong> We email you a prep brief the day before and the link to your transcript afterwards. If you gave a phone number, you get one reminder text message about two hours before the interview, sent from Mira's number through Voximplant.</li>
            <li><strong>The interview itself is recorded</strong>, and Mira says so at the start. We record each person's audio as a separate track; in the browser studio your browser also records your own microphone and uploads it to us in chunks, so a network hiccup does not cost your words. If you join from the browser studio with your camera on, we also record video (the camera is optional). If you take the interview as a phone call instead, the call is placed and recorded through Voximplant.</li>
            <li><strong>Mira hears you through xAI.</strong> Mira is a Grok voice agent run by xAI: during the interview your voice is streamed to it so she can hear you and answer in real time.</li>
            <li><strong>The transcript.</strong> We transcribe each track with Whisper, an open-source speech-recognition model that runs on the build servers our pipeline uses (GitHub Actions); the audio is not sent to a transcription service. xAI's Grok then drafts the chapter list, show notes and Mira's narration from the transcript and your application.</li>
            <li><strong>Your review.</strong> You get your own transcript with a week to approve it, cut anything from it, or refuse it; what you cut is removed from the audio. After seven days without a reply the episode publishes as sent, and a takedown stays available afterwards. Patrick Novak reviews every episode before release and is notified of each step in a private Slack channel.</li>
            <li><strong>What is published.</strong> The edited episode, the transcript of what you said, your name and the details you gave for your introduction go to the podcast feed, this site, YouTube and the show's newsletter.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Your application, booking and interview status are stored in Supabase; the recordings, separate tracks and transcript are stored in Cloudflare R2.</p>

        <h3>Information collected automatically</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Site analytics</strong> (Google Analytics 4, and optionally Plausible): pages visited, referral source, device and browser type, anonymised IP address. Google's tags load only after you accept the cookie banner; see <a href="#services">section 3</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Session cookie</strong>: when you sign in to a Nerra account, our API sets a signed cookie (valid up to 90 days) so gallery downloads and your account page work. It contains your email and a signature, nothing else.</li>
            <li><strong>Podcast listening data</strong> collected by the app you listen in, which we do not control, and anonymised download counts from OP3 (<a href="#services">section 3</a>).</li>
            <li><strong>Server logs</strong> kept by our hosting providers (GitHub Pages for the site, Cloudflare for audio, images, and the account API).</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="use">2. How we use it</h2>
        <div class="legal-table-wrap">
        <table class="legal-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Basis under PIPEDA</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Send the newsletters you subscribed to</td><td>Your explicit consent (opt-in)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Build and deliver your personal feed and member perks</td><td>Performance of the membership you bought</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Understand how the site is used and improve it</td><td>Legitimate interest, with consent for analytics cookies</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Reply to your messages</td><td>Your request</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Schedule, record, edit and publish a guest interview</td><td>Your consent, given when you apply and book, and stated again at the start of the recording</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Send account and billing notices</td><td>Contract performance</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Meet legal obligations</td><td>Legal requirement</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="services">3. Services we rely on</h2>

        <h3>Buttondown (newsletters and account emails)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Holds your email address, the shows you chose, and open/click metrics.</li>
            <li>Every email has an unsubscribe link; unsubscribing takes effect immediately.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Stripe (payments)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Processes membership and donation payments under its own privacy policy. Card details never touch our systems.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Cloudflare (audio hosting, image gallery, account API)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Serves episode audio, gallery images, and private member feeds, and runs the API behind sign-in and subscriptions. Member records (email, chosen shows, subscription status) are stored there.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>GitHub Pages (website hosting)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Serves this site and keeps standard access logs. Data may be processed in the United States.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Resend (sign-in and guest emails)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Delivers the sign-in links for your Nerra account and the emails an interview guest receives. It sees the address and the message it delivers.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Interview shows only: Supabase, Cal.com, Voximplant and xAI</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Supabase</strong> stores guest applications, bookings and the state of each interview (database hosted in Canada).</li>
            <li><strong>Cal.com</strong> handles interview booking.</li>
            <li><strong>Voximplant</strong> runs the interview room and the phone fallback, records the conversation, and sends the reminder text.</li>
            <li><strong>xAI</strong> runs Mira's live voice during the interview and the Grok model that drafts show notes from the transcript. (xAI also writes and voices the network's other shows, from public sources &mdash; that involves no personal information.)</li>
            <li>See <a href="#guests">If you are a guest</a> for what each one receives.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Google Analytics and Google Ads measurement</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>We use Google Consent Mode v2: analytics and advertising storage are <strong>denied by default</strong>. The cookie banner asks before any tracking cookie is set. "Reject" means none are.</li>
            <li>If we ever run ads, Google Ads conversion tracking would count anonymous newsletter signups from those ads. We do not run cross-site retargeting and do not target individuals.</li>
            <li>Plausible, when enabled, collects only aggregate, cookie-free traffic counts.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>OP3 (podcast download counts)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Episode audio URLs pass through <a href="https://op3.dev/about" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OP3</a>, an open-source analytics prefix that reports anonymised downloads by app, device type, and country. It does not retain personal identifiers.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Embedded content</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>The Offshore North dashboard embeds the team's public YB Tracking page and a map drawn on OpenStreetMap tiles; those services see the request your browser makes to load them.</li>
            <li>Instagram posts on that page are shown as links until you press the button to show their pictures. Only then does your browser load Instagram's own embed, and <a href="https://privacycenter.instagram.com/policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Instagram's privacy policy</a> applies to it, including its cookies.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Podcast platforms</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Apple Podcasts, Spotify, YouTube, and every other app that carries our feeds have their own privacy policies and their own listener analytics, which we do not control.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="legal-callout">
            <p><strong>We do not sell or rent personal information.</strong> Your email is shared only with the services above that need it to do the thing you asked for.</p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="retention">4. Retention</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Newsletter and account email:</strong> kept while you are subscribed or a member; deleted on unsubscribe or on request.</li>
            <li><strong>Membership records:</strong> kept while the subscription is active and for as long as needed to handle billing questions afterwards.</li>
            <li><strong>Messages:</strong> kept for 90 days after your inquiry is resolved.</li>
            <li><strong>Guest interviews:</strong> the recordings, separate tracks and transcript are kept with the published episode. Ask, and we delete the raw recordings and your application; a published episode can be taken down at any time. If you refuse your transcript or the interview is not published, ask and we delete everything we hold from it.</li>
            <li><strong>Analytics:</strong> up to 12 months, non-identifying.</li>
            <li><strong>Hosting logs:</strong> per each provider's standard retention (typically 90 days).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Deletion requests are completed within 30 days, subject to any legal retention requirement.</p>

        <h2 id="rights">5. Your rights under PIPEDA</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Access</strong> the personal information we hold about you.</li>
            <li><strong>Correct</strong> anything inaccurate or incomplete.</li>
            <li><strong>Delete</strong> your information, subject to legal requirements.</li>
            <li><strong>Withdraw consent</strong> for newsletters at any time.</li>
            <li><strong>Complain</strong> to the Office of the Privacy Commissioner of Canada.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>To exercise any of these, use the contact details in <a href="#contact">section 11</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="email">6. Email compliance (CASL)</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>We email only addresses that opted in.</li>
            <li>Every message identifies us and carries an unsubscribe link.</li>
            <li>Unsubscribe requests are honoured immediately, well inside CASL's 10-business-day limit.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="security">7. Security</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>HTTPS everywhere, including audio and API traffic.</li>
            <li>Signed session cookies that cannot be forged and expire on their own.</li>
            <li>Card data handled only by Stripe.</li>
            <li>Access to member records limited to what running the service requires.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>No method of transmission over the internet is completely secure; we cannot guarantee absolute security.</p>

        <h2 id="transfers">8. International transfers</h2>
        <p>Some providers (GitHub, Cloudflare, Buttondown, Stripe, Google, Resend, and for guests Cal.com, Voximplant and xAI) process data outside Canada. Those transfers are necessary to run the service, and by using Nerra Network you consent to them under this policy.</p>

        <h2 id="children">9. Children</h2>
        <p>Nerra Network is not directed at children under 13 and we do not knowingly collect their information. If we learn that we have, we delete it.</p>

        <h2 id="changes">10. Changes</h2>
        <p>We update this page when our practices change and revise the date at the top. Continued use after a change means you accept the updated policy.</p>

